libxtc (0.9.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Preemption: fixed a rare hang under pathological all-CPU load (a
    fiber preempted while holding a pthread_mutex deadlocked the loop);
    the whole library now dogfoods preemption-safe locks
    (__os_mutex_* + __xtc_mtx_lock/unlock).  aarch64 involuntary-
    preemption trampoline added (Graviton-validated).
  * Deterministic Simulation Testing (DST) toward FoundationDB parity:
    - seeded network-partition + message-latency simulation;
    - lock manager parks fibers so it runs under simulation;
    - the sqlxtc buffer manager runs under DST with byte-identical
      replay;
    - the WAL crash-recovery capstone: seeded crash at an arbitrary
      point recovers exactly the durable-commit set, replayable across
      a seed sweep (fork-per-run isolation);
    - fixed work-stealing completion-order non-determinism under replay;
    - more Buggify sites (channel, work-stealing) plus a steal-order gap
      found and fixed.  The sim suite is now 13 replay-verified tests.
  * sqlxtc: confirmed NO-STEAL + NO-FORCE is the correct recovery model
    (ARIES undo/CLR machinery is built and tested but dormant by
    policy); a probe proved in-place STEAL recovery is not torn-base-
    safe.  Added a WAL_MAX_REC bound so recovery rejects a torn tail
    record instead of allocating its garbage length.
  * M16.1a: in-tree mock PostgreSQL backend (examples/09_pgmock) proving
    the PG-adapter runtime seam (postmaster + backends as xtc_procs on
    the scheduler, PG-v3 SELECT 1 round-trip, zero PG source).
  * Docs: complete man-page enumeration (372 public functions; strict
    coverage gate) and the four missing subsystem pages
    (xtc_blocking/pkey/iosched/dio_sched).

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Fri, 03 Jul 2026 14:29:56 -0400

libxtc (0.8.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Preemption facility (native, inspired by CMU's Lightweight
    Preemptible Functions; see docs/M_PREEMPTION.md):
    - Phase 0: a per-worker CPU-time interval timer seam
      (xtc_preempt_arm/disarm; POSIX per-thread CPU-time timers,
      NOSYS elsewhere).
    - Phase 0.5: a per-thread async-signal-unsafe-region depth counter
      (__xtc_unsafe_enter/leave) bracketing the allocator, realizing
      libas-safe's discipline via a counter -- also hardens the fault
      handler against unwinding out of a corrupt malloc arena.
    - Phase 1 (SUPPORTED): cooperative-assisted preemption --
      xtc_exec_set_preempt(interval_ns) arms the timer per worker, and a
      tick makes xtc_yield_if_due callers yield, time-slicing a compute
      fiber with no manual budget.
    - Phase 2: the signal-context involuntary-yield infrastructure +
      safety gate (crit_depth + unsafe_depth) is in place; the resumable
      redirect declines for now (a portable whole-ucontext copy is
      unsound) and falls back to Phase 1, pending per-architecture
      mcontext surgery (Go's method).  Untrusted pure-CPU work uses
      xtc_osproc meanwhile.

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Wed, 01 Jul 2026 22:00:00 -0400

libxtc (0.7.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Deterministic Simulation Testing (DST) matured toward FoundationDB
    parity: a seeded, replayable scheduler drives the real multi-loop
    work-stealing executor; a 20,000-seed soak passes with zero
    failures across 256 distinct schedules; seeded fault injection,
    critical-section fault points, simulated I/O faults (deferred
    seeded AIO completions + short/EIO faults), and FoundationDB-style
    Buggify (pessimal-path injection in real runtime code) are all in
    the sim-dst test suite, and the fiber-yielding latches
    (xtc_amutex/xtc_arwlock) are exercised under the deterministic
    scheduler.  Wiring the I/O-fault work fixed two latent runtime bugs
    (a missing per-loop bind and a quiescence stall).
  * Concurrent B-link node merge is now enabled BY DEFAULT: emptied
    pages are merged and reclaimed even under concurrent latch-free
    deletes.  Three fixes made it correct -- descent-level dead/fence
    revalidation, a buffer-manager reclamation interlock (pin-safe
    deferred drop + quarantine + dedup-on-publish), and an
    internal-split hi-fence fix -- verified by a churn-gone gate,
    ASan, and the 32/32 SQL oracle.
  * Cooperative-assisted preemption: xtc_exec_set_preempt arms a
    per-worker CPU-time timer whose ticks make xtc_yield_if_due callers
    yield, time-slicing a long compute fiber without a manual budget
    (the first phases of a native preemption facility inspired by CMU's
    Lightweight Preemptible Functions; see docs/M_PREEMPTION.md).
  * The Tina-style stackless Isolate layer is now a supported library
    API (xtc_tnt_*): dense-arena Isolates (hundreds of bytes each, no
    per-isolate stack) for extreme fan-in, alongside the stackful
    fiber model.
  * Portability + build: qualified on musl libc with a CI job (builtin
    _Unwind_Backtrace tier, no libunwind dependency); optional DPDK
    dependency wired (configure --with-dpdk); the process registry is
    now an O(1) chained FNV-1a hash table.
  * Benchmarks: memory-per-task and fairness-under-a-runaway-task, to
    measure the stackful-fiber RAM floor and the cooperative-scheduling
    gap the preemption work closes.

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Wed, 01 Jul 2026 18:00:00 -0400

libxtc (0.6.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * sqlxtc example is now a fully libxtc-native SQL engine: the vendored
    SQLite (sqlite3.c, the VDBE, virtual tables, and the mutex/memory/
    page-cache/VFS shims) is removed.  The engine runs on a Lime SQL
    parser, a vectorized executor, and an xtc-native storage engine
    (B-link tree over a cooling buffer pool with an ARIES write-ahead
    log).  Correctness is proved by an external differential oracle
    against python's sqlite3.
  * sqlxtc performance: bounded top-N for ORDER BY ... LIMIT, and a
    binary-search membership test for IN (list) / IN (SELECT).
  * sqlxtc: the catalog, SSI, and snapshot locks moved to xtc_amutex so
    the catalog row is persisted inside the lock, closing a CREATE race.
  * io_kqueue: native file AIO restricted to FreeBSD/DragonFly; macOS
    (whose 26 SDK no longer exposes sigev_notify_kqueue) uses the
    blocking-pool offload.

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Thu, 25 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000

